course: dictionary
lesson: 14847
title: One word, four meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one to use. The word ramble is a good example. It can mean a walk, a way of talking, or a plant spreading out. «We went for a ramble through the woods on Sunday.» Here the words woods and Sunday show that a ramble is a walk.
explain: Now read «Gran rambled on about her cat for so long that we forgot the question.» Nobody went for a walk here. Gran talked for a long time and wandered away from the point. Ask yourself: does the sentence mean a walk, or talking that goes off track?
explain: Plants can ramble too. «The roses rambled all over the old garden wall.» A rose cannot walk, so rambled must mean that the roses grew and spread in every direction. Always test your meaning in the sentence to check it makes sense.
fact: Ramble can be a verb (to ramble, rambled, rambling) or a noun (a ramble).
fact: «Ramble» means to walk for pleasure in the countryside, often without a fixed route.
fact: «Ramble» can also mean to talk or write for a long time in a confused way, wandering off the point.
fact: Ramblers are people who go on walks in the countryside, and many join ramblers' clubs.
fact: The countryside code asks ramblers to close gates behind them, so farm animals stay safe.
exercise: «We went for a ramble through the woods on Sunday.» What is a ramble here? | A loud argument | A short sleep after lunch | A fast race on a bike | *A long walk in the countryside
exercise: The countryside code asks ramblers to do one thing. What is it? | *Close gates behind them | Climb over every wall | Light a big fire | Leave the gates wide open
exercise: «Gran rambled on about her cat for so long that we forgot the question.» What does rambled mean here? | Sang a happy song | Grew across the garden wall | Walked slowly up a hill | *Talked for a long time in a confused way
test: «The roses rambled all over the old garden wall.» What does rambled mean here? | Fell down and broke | *Grew and spread in all directions | Were cut short by gardeners | Were painted bright red
test: Your teacher says, «Don't ramble in your writing; stay on the point.» What does ramble mean in writing? | Write in very big letters | Write a poem about the countryside | Use lots of colours | *Wander off the point and go on too long
exam: In «We set off on a ramble», which word is a noun? | off | on | *ramble | set
